      englishTranslation:
        "1. Refutation of Objections Against the Cessation of Ignorance (Avidyā-nivṛtti-bhaṅga): The Dvaita opponent poses a dilemma: Is the cessation of Avidyā identical with Ātman (in which case it cannot be newly accomplished by spiritual effort) or distinct from Ātman (if real, it compromises non-duality; if mithyā, it falls back into Avidyā or its effects)? Madhusūdana resolves this masterfully: liberation is either Ātman as indicated/demarcated (upalakṣita) by the final intuitive cognition (carama-vṛtti)—just as a 'cook' retains the designation even after cooking ceases, or space inside a jar is said to be 'produced' via its limiting adjunct—or it is identical with that final anti-ignorance vṛtti itself, just as the destruction of disjunction (vibhāga-dhvaṁsa) resolves into the substrate once the opposing state ceases. Upholding 'Neti, Neti' ('Not this, not this') and the classic parable of discovering the gold necklace already around one's own neck (kaṇṭhagata-cāmīkara), he shows why achieving what was already intrinsically one's own bliss-nature through removing ignorance constitutes the supreme human goal. As for the 'fifth category' (pañcama-prakāra) theory of Avidyā-nivṛtti, Madhusūdana notes it is merely a pedagogical device for beginners.",
